WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.379
"Are you excited about your field trip?"

2
00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:09.168
Grandpa asked Laurie as they waited for the school bus.

3
00:00:09.169 --> 00:00:18.767
Laurie shrugged. "I guess so. We're just visiting George Washington's headquarters in New Jersey during the American Revolution.

4
00:00:18.768 --> 00:00:23.654
Then we're riding horses through some woods where Washington's soldiers camped."

5
00:00:23.655 --> 00:00:31.310
"That sounds great!" Grandpa said. "The American Revolution is my favorite part of American history.

6
00:00:31.311 --> 00:00:39.667
It was an exciting time when colonists were figuring out who they were and what they wanted from their government.

7
00:00:39.668 --> 00:00:45.198
When it was all over, they had founded a new country."

8
00:00:45.199 --> 00:00:52.709
Laurie couldn't help smiling. As usual Grandpa was enthusiastic about something in history!

9
00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:58.702
"I know the Revolution had something to do with tea and taxes," she said to him.

10
00:00:58.703 --> 00:01:02.738
"But what were the colonists really fighting about?"

11
00:01:02.739 --> 00:01:07.714
"Independence and the right to decide how they were governed.

12
00:01:07.715 --> 00:01:13.757
Remember, what we know now as American states were British colonies back then.

13
00:01:13.758 --> 00:01:18.466
The founders and original colonists had come from England.

14
00:01:18.467 --> 00:01:27.330
So everyone, even people born in America, were British citizens governed by the British king—King George III.But

15
00:01:27.331 --> 00:01:38.033
he lived in England. Many colonists started grumbling that he couldn't possibly know what was best for the colonies when he was so far away. "The

16
00:01:38.034 --> 00:01:45.602
really loud grumbling started when King George created the Stamp Act in 1765. This

17
00:01:45.603 --> 00:01:56.031
act said that any paper good sold in the colonies had to have a stamp on it, and the stamp had to be purchased from a British official. It

18
00:01:56.032 --> 00:01:59.718
was basically a tax paid to England. So

19
00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:07.372
the colonists protested this. 'No taxation without representation,' they said. That

20
00:02:07.373 --> 00:02:12.642
meant: you can't tax us unless we agree on the law together. Then

21
00:02:12.643 --> 00:02:18.822
England expanded the tax to include glass, tea, and other things. So

22
00:02:18.823 --> 00:02:24.178
the colonists decided to boycott British goods. England

23
00:02:24.179 --> 00:02:30.588
didn't like this, and sent soldiers to the colonies to keep things in order. The

24
00:02:30.589 --> 00:02:37.113
war for independence officially broke out in 1775. The

25
00:02:37.114 --> 00:02:44.464
next year the founding fathers of the United States wrote the Declaration of Independence. This

26
00:02:44.465 --> 00:02:53.456
document explained everything that the Americans were fighting for: life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness. They

27
00:02:53.457 --> 00:02:58.086
wanted to be completely free from British rule." The

28
00:02:58.087 --> 00:03:01.248
school bus rumbled up the street. "I'm

29
00:03:01.249 --> 00:03:15.275
sure you'll learn everything that happened after that on your field trip! Have fun!" At

30
00:03:15.276 --> 00:03:21.176
four o'clock, Grandpa heard feet pounding on the steps. "How

31
00:03:21.177 --> 00:03:26.341
was it?" Grandpa asked as his granddaughter bounced into the room. "So

32
00:03:26.342 --> 00:03:34.743
much fun!" Laurie answered. "I got you something." She held out a brown paper bag. "Thanks!"

33
00:03:34.744 --> 00:03:38.648
"It's

34
00:03:38.649 --> 00:03:44.138
a quill pen and some ink." Laurie showed him how to use it. "First,

35
00:03:44.139 --> 00:03:51.205
cut the tip of the quill with a knife to make a slanted edge. Then,

36
00:03:51.206 --> 00:03:56.903
dip that end in the ink bottle. That's how the colonists wrote. Cool,

37
00:03:56.904 --> 00:04:02.311
huh? The park ranger who gave us our tour taught me that." "I

38
00:04:02.312 --> 00:04:05.380
bet you learned a lot today. Now

39
00:04:05.381 --> 00:04:12.911
you probably know more than I do about George Washington and the American Revolution," Grandpa said, grinning. "No!"

40
00:04:12.912 --> 00:04:21.404
Laurie protested. "But I did learn that George Washington was a plantation owner from Virginia. When

41
00:04:21.405 --> 00:04:28.349
the colonists gathered to fight the British, they started an army called the Continental Army. George

42
00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:31.746
Washington became the commander. He

43
00:04:31.747 --> 00:04:41.338
led the soldiers through almost ten years of fighting, moving from colony to colony, until all the important areas were won. When

44
00:04:41.339 --> 00:04:48.491
the war ended, the colonists, who were now Americans, asked him to be their first president." "I

45
00:04:48.492 --> 00:04:52.686
couldn't have said it better," Grandpa said proudly. "Now

46
00:04:52.687 --> 00:04:59.883
tell me something else you learned today," he said. "Something that really interested you." Laurie

47
00:04:59.884 --> 00:05:04.796
thought a moment. "The story of Tempe Wick." "Tempe

48
00:05:04.797 --> 00:05:10.302
Wick? That sounds like a story I don't know." "Tempe

49
00:05:10.303 --> 00:05:17.606
Wick was a colonial girl who lived on her family's farm, called Wick Farm, in New Jersey. Tempe

50
00:05:17.607 --> 00:05:22.433
loved horses and wasn't afraid of anything. She

51
00:05:22.434 --> 00:05:27.120
was also a proud patriot who believed in the Revolution. "During

52
00:05:27.121 --> 00:05:33.448
the war, Washington sent thirteen thousand soldiers to the woods around Wick Farm. They

53
00:05:33.449 --> 00:05:37.607
cleared the land and built log cabins to live in. But

54
00:05:37.608 --> 00:05:41.804
winter in New Jersey was cold and snowy. The

55
00:05:41.805 --> 00:05:46.587
soldiers were freezing, hungry, and tired. The

56
00:05:46.588 --> 00:05:52.270
war seemed like it would never end. Sometimes the soldiers were bad. They

57
00:05:52.271 --> 00:05:58.445
stole food from nearby farms, and demanded that people give their best horses to them. "One

58
00:05:58.446 --> 00:06:05.933
day Tempe was out riding her favorite horse, Colonel, when some Continental soldiers stopped her. They

59
00:06:05.934 --> 00:06:11.106
had just rebelled against their general and were abandoning their camp. They

60
00:06:11.107 --> 00:06:14.647
told her that Colonel was a beautiful horse. Then

61
00:06:14.648 --> 00:06:18.734
they grabbed his reins and said they wanted him! But

62
00:06:18.735 --> 00:06:22.158
Tempe didn't give in to their bullying. She

63
00:06:22.159 --> 00:06:25.113
didn't even get off her horse. As

64
00:06:25.114 --> 00:06:29.125
soon as they let go of the reins, she galloped away! "Tempe

65
00:06:29.126 --> 00:06:32.355
knew the soldiers were following her. She

66
00:06:32.356 --> 00:06:37.382
was sure they'd come to the farm and take Colonel away. So

67
00:06:37.383 --> 00:06:41.461
she made a plan to fool the soldiers. Instead

68
00:06:41.462 --> 00:06:47.306
of putting Colonel in the stable, she led him into her bedroom! When

69
00:06:47.307 --> 00:06:52.435
the soldiers came to search the farm, they never thought to look in the house. The

70
00:06:52.436 --> 00:06:58.734
soldiers left empty-handed, and Tempe Wick got to keep her dear Colonel." Laurie

71
00:06:58.735 --> 00:07:05.066
smiled. "And I got to see her beautiful red cape! The

72
00:07:05.067 --> 00:07:08.502
very same cape she wore that winter. I

73
00:07:08.503 --> 00:07:14.283
can just imagine her riding through the woods like Little Red Riding Hood." Grandpa

74
00:07:14.284 --> 00:07:24.209
laughed loudly and clapped. "That's a great story!" he said. "I

75
00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:28.525
like it because it's about a young girl like you. She

76
00:07:28.526 --> 00:07:36.882
was proud to support the Continental soldiers, but she was also brave enough to stand up to them when they did something wrong." "Kind

77
00:07:36.883 --> 00:07:41.764
of like how the colonists stood up to the British," Laurie said. "They

78
00:07:41.765 --> 00:07:47.488
were courageous, proud of their colonies, and fought to protect their rights." "Exactly,"

79
00:07:47.489 --> 00:07:54.025
Grandpa agreed. "You told that story very well, Laurie." "Thanks,

80
00:07:54.026 --> 00:07:56.409
Grandpa." "No,

81
00:07:56.410 --> 00:08:02.701
thank you —my little historian."

